  "headline": "Bonds reopen and sell off as stocks rally: growth today, not my squeeze, but gold held",
  "body": [
    "The Treasury market came back and gave me the one answer I did not test for: no rally at all. The whole curve sold off about 9 basis points while the S&P 500 rose 1.6%, and the VIX fell to 23.8, down a third on the week. Yields rising with stocks rising is growth being repriced, not a financing squeeze, so on the day the fiscal-dominance signature is absent and I say so plainly.",
    "Yesterday I called the dollar's slip a failure to hold a high; today it is at 104.0, 96% of its range, and it rallied on a risk-on day. That I got wrong: the dollar is winning this month, up 1.8%. What held is the part that matters more to me. Gold rose 0.4% into a stronger dollar and a 9 basis-point rise in the 10-year, which is the bid under pressure the thesis needs. The $3,700 bar is still not cleared at $3,668 and the VIX is under 25, so I did not add.",
    "Day against month, I trust the month. Over the month the 5-year fell 12 basis points and the 30-year only 4, so the long end refused to rally with the front and the 30-minus-5 spread steepened 9 basis points to 58. MOVE at 95, down 10% on the week, says the bond market is relaxed about today's selloff; a relaxed selloff is growth, a stressed one is financing, and one calm risk-on session does not unbuild a month of term premium.",
    "Bitcoin is the outlier: down 2.5% on a day stocks rose 1.6%, sitting at 4% of its range. That is neither a liquidity hedge nor a risk asset, it is being sold regardless of mood, and one bitcoin now buys 16.3 ounces of gold, down 17% on the month. Copper slipped 0.7% and the copper/gold ratio is down 7% on the month, so today's growth bid did not reach the metal that would confirm it. Brent at $91, down 18% on the month, keeps the oil leg dormant; the rupee is up 2.9% against the dollar over the month and paying for no shock. None of today's headlines has a channel to these markets.",
    "I hold gold, keep the dollar hedge that paid today, and keep the 10-year at 4.94% even though it cost me 9 basis points; the 30-year at 5.33% and bitcoin stay out. I add to gold on a weekly close above $3,700 with the VIX back above 25, and the dollar through its 52-week high of 104.32 with gold under $3,450 ends the thesis. The test now is the slope: if stocks and yields keep rising together and the 30-minus-5 spread narrows from 58, this is a boom, not fiscal dominance, and the gold money goes to copper instead."
  ],
